From 1efb80b4ec31f3fbf1daeb98a7f52616e225f6ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aamir <aamir@tallow-lang.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:06:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/27] codegen+runtime: bind StructuredData.extract natively
 (P2.T1)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The last unbound schema-first native now has all three legs: the interpreter arm
(already correct, interp.rs:9959), the FFI, and the codegen lowering — so the two
engines read one HTML document the same way instead of one falling back.

The FFI returns the serialized `open_graph` through the return slot and writes
`json_ld` + `has_json_ld` through out-params. That is not a preference: it is the
`tallow_crawler_from_links` split (the one computed string out the front, the rest
out-param), which keeps refusal propagation on the path codegen already has —
`emit_runtime_string_null_check`. No new IR failure machinery, so nothing new has to
be trusted.

Two measurements changed the plan's own specification:

1. The planned `*mut *mut c_char` for json_ld could not work. A runtime array has
   exactly one shape (`native_array`: `[count][slot...]` as a boxed `[i64]`), built
   inside producers; there is no public constructor, so a raw C array could never
   become a value `tallow_array_len`/`tallow_array_get` accept. The native builds it
   with the same helper the other string-array producers use.

2. `open_graph` could not be a Map. Codegen has no Map value at all — no `%struct.Map`,
   no `map_alloc`, no `MapEntry`, no `tallow_map_*` symbol anywhere — and the layout's
   `field_kinds["open_graph"] = "Map"` tag had no consumer. The field is therefore
   declared `String` and carries `key<TAB>value` lines in document order. Changing that
   tag removes no behaviour because nothing read it; it stops the layout claiming a
   shape the backend cannot build. The divergence against the interpreter (which holds
   a real Value::Map) is documented in the FFI header rather than discovered later.

`has_json_ld` is stored by hand: it is the only struct field anywhere that is genuinely
i1, and `emit_store_i64_field` would write eight bytes into one bit.

Verified: cargo check --lib --bins RC=0; codegen suite 621 passed/0 failed;
runtime::structured_data_ffi 7 passed/0 (real array shape, both refusals, document
order, attribute-order independence, multiple JSON-LD blocks); then the CLI under
--require-native — a flag whose documented contract is to refuse the interpreter
fallback — which exited 0 and printed true/1/false/0 for a page with JSON-LD and one
without, over a native link step visible on stderr. The same flag on the same binary
exited 1 with "native build unavailable" when the probe was broken, which is the
contrast showing the values came from the AOT path and not a silent fallback.

That first probe attempt was instructive: its JSON-LD body contained braces, Tallow read
them as string interpolation, the emitted IR referenced an undefined global, clang
failed, and --require-native refused instead of quietly interpreting. The flag caught a
real defect in the test before the test could report a false pass.

Scope note on this diff: `codegen.rs` is deliberately NOT run through rustfmt here. The
first version of this commit did, and rustfmt reformatted a file that was never
format-clean, producing 55 hunks and 184 deletions around a four-site change — unrelated
churn that would have buried the review. The formatting was reverted and the four edits
re-applied against the baseline, so this diff is 4 hunks: the declare, the layout tag,
the dispatch branch, and the arm. The native probe above ran against a binary built
before that revert; rustfmt is token-preserving, so the code is identical, and both
suites were re-run on the un-formatted file to confirm it.

Known lint gap observed while proving this (not fixed here): the resolver warns
"possibly undefined variable `StructuredData`" for a struct-static call that then
compiles and runs correctly.
